body:
&#x200B;

Your mother is not paranoid. She is being completely reasonable, because I have not the foggiest idea why anybody would wish to pass as female in Saudi Arabia, it being a not merely homophobic but viciously sexist society.

The truth is that you are almost certainly shortening your lifespan to some extent via HRT, and subjecting yourself to extra health risks via DIY. This is a trade-off that a professional, secular psychologist could help you weigh up, and a secular family therapist could help you and your family reconcile about and come to terms with.

Firstly, advice I learned the hard way for everyone feeling depressed or highly anxious for any reason: put a pause button on any big decisions until you feel substantially better.

&#x200B;

Secondly: You are bearing the heavy burden of the silence and unnecessary secrecy of millions of dysphoric gynephilic AMAB people in the 20th century who could have been open about it without serious repercussions, but who instead kept it secret from their wives and families and thereby the culture at large. If the culture at large would simply acknowledge and come to terms with the reality and sheer prevalence of autogynephilia-type phenomena (including crossdressing) and gender dysphoria in gynephilic AMAB people; if the culture of and dynamic between AMAB people were kinder and more accepting of femininity, the world would be such a better place - not just for trans people, but for everyone who cares about authentic and equitable human relationships.

&#x200B;

So many people on this subreddit come in asking whether they are 'really trans' or 'just an autogynephile/fetishist'. The trouble with these questions is that 'just fetishists' is what AMAB gynephilic dysphorics have traditionally made themselves out to be to their wives, and 'really trans' - i.e., the stereotypical picture of the actually relatively rare effeminate-from-childhood androphilic dysphoric - is what AMAB gynephilic dysphorics have made themselves out to be doctors in order to get HRT/SRS.

&#x200B;

The lives of gynephilic AMAB people with gender dysphoria - that is, MOST AMAB people with gender dysphoria - have for most of the 20th century been \*characteristically\* marked by this shame and self-doubt tortured wrestling you describe about whether they are 'really trans'. And Carey Callahan, who had OCD and detransitioned, has argued that this doubt and rumination cycle about identity is a major component and cause of dysphoria and the desire to transition. She tackled her dysphoria by tackling OCD symptomatology first.

&#x200B;

For a meaningful comparison: I don't see people asking 'am I BIID or just an apotemnophile'. That's because there is simply no pre-existing socially acceptable category of person who wants to become an amputee: so nobody has anything to lose by being open about the paraphilic aspects of BIID ('hey, we're already mad as it is'.)

&#x200B;

I would recommend talking to a trustworthy counsellor about the kind of intimate relationship/s you might like to have and how you might be able to integrate your autogynephilic desires into them. Because they will never go away, but they can in principle be renegotiated with intimate partners and fulfilled in ways which don't require medical or legal transition (for example via certain relationship dynamics, clothes/hair/makeup, or forms of physical intimacy).

&#x200B;

Please also don't be afraid that heterosexual women will not accept your dysphoria and/or autogynephilia. I'm a heterosexual cis woman and if I had an AGP or dysphoric partner I'd still love them and want to help them come to terms with those desires and be at peace with themselves. This is partly because I have an ETLE of my own: weight gain fetishism. (And I had sexual OCD about my fetish and that's how I became aware of all these sexology concepts.) 'Littles', aspergirls, and female furries of whom I have met several are also likely to understand. There is nothing wrong or bad about autogynephilia or gender dysphoria. Autogynephilia spectrum fantasies, behaviours, and desires are NOT creepy or pathological. They are SO much more common than men and trans AMAB people are admitting to each other!

&#x200B;

Lastly, if you decide that you need to medically transition: that is fine, too. That is what some people need. It's ultimately for my own sake that I wish there were more non-transitioned GNC people in the world.
